-- title:
--   Galois action on pⁿ-torsion of J₀(M) factors through a finite level
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a natural number that is nonzero and let $p$ be a prime. The assertion is that for every natural number $n$ there is an intermediate field $L$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ inside $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (the chosen `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`) which is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb{Q}$ and has the following property: for every $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ that fixes every element of $L$, and every $v$ in `JZero M`, if $v$ lies in `Pic0.torsion` for the index $p^{n}$ then $\sigma \bullet v = v$. Here `JZero M` is the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the field `modularFunctionFieldBar M` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, that is, the quotient of the group of finitely supported $\mathbb{Z}$-valued functions on the places of that field which have degree zero by the subgroup of principal divisors; `modularFunctionFieldBar M` is obtained by adjoining to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, inside the Laurent series field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the coefficientwise image of the subfield `modularFunctionFieldFull M` of $\mathbb{Q}$-Laurent series generated by the divisor expansions at level $M$. The subgroup `Pic0.torsion ... (p ^ n)` consists of the classes annihilated by $p^{n}$ acting as an integer scalar, and the action of $\sigma$ is the natural action of $\mathrm{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ on these classes. Of the primality of $p$ the proof uses only that $p > 0$.
--
--   This records that the Galois action on the $p^{n}$-torsion of the modular Jacobian $J_0(M)$ is trivial on an open subgroup, so that each torsion level is defined over a number field; it is the finiteness input needed to regard the $p$-adic Tate module of $J_0(M)$ as a continuous Galois representation. It is used in the construction and analysis of the $\ell$-adic representations attached to newforms, for instance in the statements on characteristic polynomials of Frobenius and on inertia at primes dividing the level.
